abstract: >-
  Local AI systems require an evidence chain from kernel behavior to
  application execution, model quality, and physical device operation.
  This technical report examines the ThoxOS program with ThoxInux, its
  Ginux-derived, Rust-first x86-64 foundation. We reconcile an earlier
  Linux-based design with current source and release records,
  distinguish reusable component evidence from integrated-system
  evidence, and define a falsifiable validation protocol. The reviewed
  records document engineering-preview EFI releases and bounded
  virtual-machine lifecycle tests. They also record resident-agent
  faults in an edition payload, conditional build reproducibility, and
  incomplete Linux ABI behavior. Published language-model evaluations
  carry additional restrictions on scoring scope, serving templates,
  quantization, and hardware applicability. We formalize the separation
  between service entitlement and action authorization, and specify
  experiments for workload compatibility, agent lifecycle, offline
  operation, update recovery, and device feasibility. The review
  contributes a revision-bound evidence map and an explicit set of
  integration tests; it does not introduce a new kernel, report a
  performance advantage, or establish production security. Historical
  unit-test counts, repository-reported runs, publisher-reported model
  scores, and unexecuted protocols remain distinct. The resulting
  architecture draft supports continued implementation while identifying
  the measurements required for a complete empirical systems paper.
keywords:
  - ThoxOS
  - ThoxInux
  - local-first AI
  - operating systems
  - Rust
  - validation protocol
